      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/">T Series 10</a> is the like-for-like
          replacement: the same motor rating, belt length and width, incline range and top speed,
          with a 10-inch screen in place of 7 inches, a 325 lb rating on NordicTrack's listing, and
          a warranty you would actually hold. The
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-treadmill/">Commercial 1250</a> was the same
          $1,599.99 at NordicTrack on 14 October 2026 and adds a 22-inch-wide belt, decline to -3%,
          a 3.6 CHP rating and a 400 lb capacity, so at those prices it is the better buy for anyone
          who runs. The
          <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/">Commercial 1750</a> adds a 16-inch
          screen and a 4.25 CHP rating for $400 more.
        </p>
        <p>
          The C 1650, the C 990's step-up sibling, is also gone from NordicTrack's US site.
          Independent reviews give it the same 20 x 60-inch belt, 0 to 12% incline and 12 mph top
          speed, with a 10-inch screen and a 3.5 CHP rating. Between a used C 990 and a used
          C 1650, the condition of the individual machine matters more than the model. Against a new
          T Series 10, a used C 990 gives you similar hardware for less money, without a warranty
          and with an older console, and it only makes sense at a price that reflects both.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
